  "prompt": "What is the period of $y = \\tan(3\\theta)$, in degrees?",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "60",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "Start from the period of plain tangent — and it is NOT $360^\\circ$.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "Start from the period of plain tangent — and it is NOT $360^\\circ$."
    },
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "title": "🪜 Try a simpler one",
      "text": "Warm up with $y = \\tan(2\\theta)$: start from the tangent period $180^\\circ$ and divide by $2$ to get $90^\\circ$. Same recipe, just with $3$ instead of $2$."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "$y = \\tan\\theta$ has period $180^\\circ$; a coefficient $B$ inside divides that period by $B$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$\\frac{180^\\circ}{3} = 60^\\circ$."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "The period of $y = \\tan\\theta$ is $180^\\circ$, half that of sine and cosine.",
    "For $y = \\tan(B\\theta)$ the period becomes $\\frac{180^\\circ}{B}$.",
    "With $B = 3$ the period is $\\frac{180^\\circ}{3} = 60^\\circ$."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Track the asymptotes",
      "steps": [
        "$\\tan(3\\theta)$ is undefined when $3\\theta = 90^\\circ$, so $\\theta = 30^\\circ$.",
        "The next one is at $3\\theta = 270^\\circ$, so $\\theta = 90^\\circ$.",
        "Consecutive asymptotes are $60^\\circ$ apart, and one full branch fits between them — the period is $60^\\circ$."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "⚠️ The trap here",
      "steps": [
        "The trap is answering $120^\\circ$ by computing $\\frac{360^\\circ}{3}$ out of habit.",
        "That formula belongs to sine and cosine. Tangent starts from $180^\\circ$, not $360^\\circ$.",
        "Before dividing by $B$, always ask which function you are in: sine and cosine start at $360^\\circ$, tangent and cotangent start at $180^\\circ$."
      ]
    }
  ],
